#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The termination gate: no engagement ends without the stakeholder having been asked.

Usage: scripts/check-epic-signoff <ITEM-ID> [--root .] [--resolving <ITEM>:<from>-><to>]
                                  [--pipeline <path>]

Epic Definition of Done DE7 **and DE8**. On anything that is not an epic this passes and says so
— the gate runs on every `review-close` execution and only an engagement's ending is its
business.

On an epic it requires a question on the epic with:

  * `kind: sign-off`
  * `addressed-to: human`  — nobody else can accept on the stakeholder's behalf
  * a reply: `status: answered`, with a non-empty `## Answer`
  * `created` no earlier than the moment the engagement reached **rest**
  * a `## Question` naming **every child item of the epic**, by ID

The last two are what generalise F-022's fix into a termination gate.

**Rest, not closure.** F-022 gated `open -> done`. An epic with a `blocked` child never reaches
`done`, so the run that most needed the gate — one that ended at an impasse — never evaluated it,
and the stakeholder went looking for the question and wrote down that it never came (F-045). The
trigger is now rest: every child stopped, no question open, no request open. `scripts/lib/
engagement.py` decides it, and the orchestrator reads the same function, so the two cannot
disagree about whether an engagement is over.

**Naming every child.** "List what was not delivered" cannot be checked; "name every child" can.
It is also the fix for the second half of the gap: a bug the pipeline filed and never fixed is a
child, so it is in the statement whether or not anyone remembered it. A stakeholder found one for
themselves after a run ended — *"there's also a bug sitting at `planned`… that I was never told
about"* (F-046).

**DE8: the one question that was not about our agenda.** Somewhere in the engagement, before the
sign-off, the stakeholder was asked an open question — `kind: elicitation` — and answered it.
Iteration 3's stakeholder had two real wants in persona for a whole engagement and no question
ever created a vehicle for them: *"What I never got asked about was anything I would have thought
to say myself"* (F-064). This is presence-checkable and nothing more: it cannot know whether the
question was a good one. `review-close` may file the elicitation alongside the sign-off, so the
rule cannot deadlock an engagement that forgot it — and the record shows which run asked early.

**Deferral.** An engagement may end at the **impasse** (`open -> blocked`) on a deferred
acknowledgment — the stakeholder replied "not yet", and parking it is the honest record. It may
not *close* on one: `open -> done` requires `status: answered`. Both moves are made from `open`,
so the gate is told the pending move through `--resolving`, which `run-gate` passes it. With no
`--resolving` the strict rule applies, because a gate that relaxes when nobody told it anything
is a gate that checked nothing (F-033).

**E4 by silence — the ending where no reply ever arrives.** A stakeholder who simply stops
answering reaches no ending at all under the rule above, because the rule requires a reply. So the
gate has a second accepting branch, and it is the same criterion in its E4 form: *asked, and the
ask stood unanswered for the threshold* (`dor-dod.md` DE7, `spec/ids-and-statuses.md` §3.5a). It
requires all of:

  * `tracker/waiting/<EP-ID>.md` shows at least `termination.silence.threshold_rounds` trailing
    halts sharing one `inbound` digest — the count read from the same
    `scripts/lib/engagement.py` the orchestrator and `engagement-state` read, and the threshold
    read from `pipeline.yaml`, which is the single source all three consult;
  * an ask that went unanswered: a question addressed to `human` that is `open` or `abandoned`.
    **Ending while never having asked stays illegal** — E4 permits an ask with an empty
    `## Answer`, never a missing ask;
  * no sign-off left `open`. If one was filed it is `abandoned` with an **empty** `## Answer`,
    which is what distinguishes E4 from E3 in the record: E3 has the stakeholder's own words
    verbatim, E4 has the emptiness and a log of the occasions on which nothing came. Where the
    silence began before rest there is no sign-off at all, and that is legal;
  * `## Ending statement` in the epic's `artifacts/review.md`, naming **every child by ID** —
    the same containment check the sign-off's `## Question` gets, for F-046's reason, because at
    E4 there is nobody to address and the statement becomes a document rather than a question;
  * DE8's elicitation, which at E4 may itself be `abandoned` with an empty `## Answer`.

**DE8 at the other three endings.** An elicitation is `blocking: false` and stops nothing
(ADR-0012), so an engagement can now reach its ending with one still unanswered — a state that
could not exist while every open question held rest. DE5 then requires it closed, and the only
honest closure for a question nobody replied to is `abandoned`. So this gate accepts an
`abandoned` elicitation at **any** ending, and asks for the half DE8 never had in exchange: the
waiting log must show that the question was actually put in front of the person. `review-close`
may file the elicitation alongside the sign-off, and without this that route was one halt away
from theatre — file it, close it, satisfy DE8, show nobody anything. The evidence exists by
construction, because rest files a sign-off, a sign-off is blocking, and a blocking ask is
reached through a halt that records every question it surfaced.

F-022: two consecutive runs closed an epic with no sign-off ever addressed to the human. The
Definition of Done gates all held — they check the record, and the record only holds what the
stakeholder said when last consulted. One of those runs had, three items earlier, negotiated a
redesign with explicit consent; closure itself still asked nothing, so a stakeholder with one
more concern had no gate at which to raise it.

Standard library only (ADR-0002).
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import os
import sys

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(HERE, "lib"))

import engagement as engagement_lib  # noqa: E402
import frontmatter  # noqa: E402
from workspace import Workspace, resolve_root  # noqa: E402


def answer_text(body: str) -> str:
    """The text under `## Answer`, up to the next level-2 heading."""
    lines = body.split("\n")
    collecting, collected = False, []
    for line in lines:
        if line.startswith("## "):
            if collecting:
                break
            collecting = line.strip().lower() == "## answer"
            continue
        if collecting:
            stripped = line.strip()
            if stripped and not stripped.startswith("<!--"):
                collected.append(stripped)
    return "\n".join(collected).strip()


def surfaced_at_a_halt(rows, holder_id: str, question_id: str) -> bool:
    """Did any recorded halt put this question in front of the person?

    The waiting log's `surfaced` column is the record of what a halt showed
    (`spec/workspace-layout.md` §1.4). It is written by `scripts/record-halt` on a different
    pass from the one that closes the question, which is what makes it evidence rather than an
    assertion by the closing execution about itself.
    """
    wanted = f"{holder_id}/{question_id}"
    return any(wanted in row.surfaced for row in rows)


def question_text(body: str) -> str:
    """The text under `## Question`, up to the next level-2 heading."""
    collecting, collected = False, []
    for line in body.split("\n"):
        if line.startswith("## "):
            if collecting:
                break
            collecting = line.strip().lower() == "## question"
            continue
        if collecting:
            collected.append(line)
    return "\n".join(collected)


def section_text(body: str, heading: str) -> str:
    """The text under a level-2 heading, up to the next one. Case-insensitive on the heading."""
    collecting, collected = False, []
    for line in body.split("\n"):
        if line.startswith("## "):
            if collecting:
                break
            collecting = line.strip().lower() == heading.lower()
            continue
        if collecting:
            collected.append(line)
    return "\n".join(collected)


def main(argv: list) -> int:
    if any(token in ("--help", "-h") for token in argv[1:]):
        print(__doc__.strip())
        return 0
    positional, options = [], {}
    index = 1
    while index < len(argv):
        token = argv[index]
        if token.startswith("--"):
            if index + 1 >= len(argv):
                print(f"check-epic-signoff: {token} needs a value", file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
            options[token[2:]] = argv[index + 1]
            index += 2
            continue
        positional.append(token)
        index += 1
    if not positional:
        print("check-epic-signoff: usage: check-epic-signoff <ITEM-ID> [--root .] "
              "[--resolving <ITEM>:<from>-><to>] [--pipeline <path>]", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    item_id = positional[0]
    root = resolve_root(options.get("root"), tool="check-epic-signoff")

    workspace = Workspace(root).load()
    item = workspace.items.get(item_id)
    if item is None:
        print(f"check-epic-signoff: {item_id} is not an item in {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if item.fields.get("type") != "epic":
        print(f"check-epic-signoff: {item_id} is a {item.fields.get('type')!r}, not an epic — "
              f"the termination gate applies to an engagement's ending only. PASS.")
        return 0

    # Which ending is pending? `run-gate` passes the move on behalf of `transition`. Absent it,
    # apply the strict rule: a gate that relaxes because nobody told it anything has stopped
    # being a gate (F-033).
    pending_to = ""
    resolving = options.get("resolving") or ""
    if "->" in resolving:
        pending_to = resolving.split("->", 1)[1].split("+")[0].strip()
    deferral_is_enough = pending_to == "blocked"

    engagement = engagement_lib.state(workspace, item,
                                      pipeline_path=options.get("pipeline"))
    expected_children = [child.identifier for child in engagement.children]

    directory = os.path.join(item.root, "questions")
    sign_offs = []
    if os.path.isdir(directory):
        for name in sorted(os.listdir(directory)):
            if not name.endswith(".md"):
                continue
            path = os.path.join(directory, name)
            fields, body, _ = frontmatter.load_file(path)
            if (fields or {}).get("kind") == "sign-off":
                sign_offs.append((path, fields or {}, body or ""))

    boundary = engagement.rest_since
    problems = []
    accepted = None
    for path, fields, body in sign_offs:
        relative = os.path.relpath(path, root)
        if fields.get("addressed-to") != "human":
            problems.append(f"{relative} is addressed to {fields.get('addressed-to')!r}; "
                            f"only the stakeholder can accept the epic")
            continue
        status = fields.get("status")
        if status not in ("answered", "deferred"):
            problems.append(f"{relative} is still {status!r} — the engagement waits on the "
                            f"stakeholder, which is the point of the gate")
            continue
        if status == "deferred" and not deferral_is_enough:
            problems.append(f"{relative} is deferred — the stakeholder replied 'not yet'. An "
                            f"engagement may be parked at the impasse on a deferral "
                            f"(open → blocked); it may not close on one")
            continue
        if not answer_text(body):
            problems.append(f"{relative} says {status} but its '## Answer' is empty")
            continue
        if boundary and str(fields.get("created") or "") < boundary:
            problems.append(f"{relative} was filed at {fields.get('created')}, before the "
                            f"engagement reached rest at {boundary}; the stakeholder was asked "
                            f"about something other than what they are being asked to accept")
            continue
        asked = question_text(body)
        unnamed = [child for child in expected_children if child not in asked]
        if unnamed:
            problems.append(f"{relative} does not name {', '.join(unnamed)} in its "
                            f"'## Question'; the statement must name every child item as "
                            f"delivered or not, so that what was not delivered is in front of "
                            f"the stakeholder rather than implied by its absence (F-046)")
            continue
        accepted = relative

    # E4 by silence. Reached only when no sign-off carries a reply, because a stakeholder who
    # replied is not absent. What stands in the reply's place is the count — and the count is
    # read from the same library the orchestrator and `engagement-state` read, against the
    # threshold `pipeline.yaml` states, so that no two of the three can disagree about whether
    # the engagement is over (F-045, spec/ids-and-statuses.md §3.5a).
    silence = None
    if accepted is None and engagement.threshold is not None \
            and engagement.silent_rounds >= engagement.threshold:
        silence_problems = []
        # The ask. E4 permits an ask that was never answered; it never permits a missing ask.
        asks = [name for name, question in engagement_lib.human_questions(workspace, item)
                if question.fields.get("status") in ("open", "abandoned")]
        if not asks:
            silence_problems.append(
                "no question addressed to the human is open or abandoned in this engagement — "
                "E4 permits an ask that stood unanswered, never an ending at which nobody was "
                "asked anything (DE7, F-022)")
        for path, fields, body in sign_offs:
            relative = os.path.relpath(path, root)
            if fields.get("status") == "open":
                silence_problems.append(
                    f"{relative} is still 'open'; a sign-off that outlived the threshold is "
                    f"closed as 'abandoned' with an empty '## Answer' before the ending is "
                    f"recorded, or next step 3 halts the whole workspace on an abandoned "
                    f"engagement's leftovers (spec/question.md §2)")
            elif fields.get("status") == "abandoned" and answer_text(body):
                silence_problems.append(
                    f"{relative} is 'abandoned' and its '## Answer' is not empty. Nothing "
                    f"arrived, so the emptiness is the evidence; writing anything there is the "
                    f"fiction the status exists to prevent (spec/question.md §2)")
        # The ending statement, which is a document because there is nobody to address.
        review_path = item.artifact_path("review.md")
        statement = ""
        if os.path.isfile(review_path):
            with open(review_path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
                statement = section_text(handle.read(), "## Ending statement")
        if not statement.strip():
            silence_problems.append(
                f"{os.path.relpath(review_path, root)} carries no '## Ending statement'. At E4 "
                f"there is nobody to address, so the statement the sign-off would have carried "
                f"becomes a document — the goal in the stakeholder's own terms, every child by "
                f"ID with its class, the silence itself, and each success measure "
                f"(spec/ids-and-statuses.md §3.5a)")
        else:
            unnamed = [child for child in expected_children if child not in statement]
            if unnamed:
                silence_problems.append(
                    f"the '## Ending statement' does not name {', '.join(unnamed)}; it must "
                    f"name every child by ID, because \"list what was not delivered\" cannot be "
                    f"checked and \"name every child\" can (F-046)")
        if silence_problems:
            problems.extend(silence_problems)
        else:
            silence = (f"{engagement.silent_rounds} silent round(s) against a threshold of "
                       f"{engagement.threshold}")

    if accepted is None and silence is None:
        print(f"check-epic-signoff: FAIL — {item_id} has no usable sign-off:", file=sys.stderr)
        for problem in problems:
            print(f"  - {problem}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    # DE8. The elicitation may live on the epic or on any child — an open question asked at
    # refinement is worth more than one asked at the ending, so the gate does not care where it
    # was filed, only that it exists in this engagement and got a reply. At E4 it may itself have
    # gone unanswered: the criterion is then that it was asked and stood unanswered, never that
    # it was skipped (dor-dod.md DE8).
    elicitations, unsurfaced = [], []
    waiting_rows = engagement_lib.read_waiting_log(root, item.identifier)
    for holder in [item] + list(engagement.children):
        for question in holder.questions:
            if question.fields.get("kind") != "elicitation":
                continue
            status = question.fields.get("status")
            if status == "open":
                continue
            if status == "abandoned":
                if answer_text(question.body):
                    continue
                if silence is None and not surfaced_at_a_halt(
                        waiting_rows, holder.identifier, question.identifier):
                    unsurfaced.append(
                        f"{holder.identifier}/{question.identifier} is 'abandoned' but "
                        f"tracker/waiting/{item.identifier}.md never surfaced it")
                    continue
            elif not answer_text(question.body):
                continue
            elicitations.append(os.path.relpath(question.path, root))
    if not elicitations:
        for complaint in unsurfaced:
            print(f"check-epic-signoff: FAIL — {complaint}.\n"
                  f"  An elicitation closed as 'abandoned' says nobody replied, and outside E4 "
                  f"by silence the record\n"
                  f"  must also show that somebody was asked: the question's "
                  f"<ITEM>/<Q-ID> in the 'surfaced' column of\n"
                  f"  a halt. Filing an elicitation and closing it in the same execution shows "
                  f"nobody anything\n"
                  f"  (dor-dod.md DE8, ADR-0012 §4.1).", file=sys.stderr)
        if unsurfaced:
            return 1
        print(f"check-epic-signoff: FAIL — nobody asked {item_id}'s stakeholder an open "
              f"question.\n"
              f"  Epic Definition of Done DE8: at least once in an engagement, file a question "
              f"with\n"
              f"  kind: elicitation, addressed-to: human, blocking: false, asking what matters "
              f"to them\n"
              f"  that we have not asked about — and record the answer. Every other question in "
              f"this\n"
              f"  protocol comes from our list. A stakeholder in a real run held two real "
              f"requirements\n"
              f"  for a whole engagement because no question ever made room for them (F-064).\n"
              f"  It may be filed now, alongside the sign-off; earlier is worth more.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    if silence is not None:
        print(f"check-epic-signoff: PASS — {item_id} ends at E4 by silence: {silence}, and the "
              f"'## Ending statement' in artifacts/review.md names all "
              f"{len(expected_children)} child item(s). No reply arrived and none is claimed; "
              f"DE8 satisfied by {elicitations[0]}")
        return 0

    if not sign_offs:
        print(f"check-epic-signoff: FAIL — {item_id} has no sign-off question.\n"
              f"  Epic Definition of Done DE7: before an epic closes, file a question on it with\n"
              f"  kind: sign-off, addressed-to: human, blocking: true — restating the goal, "
              f"naming\n"
              f"  EVERY child item as delivered or not with one line of why, and asking plainly "
              f"whether\n"
              f"  the stakeholder accepts the engagement as it stands. Then suspend the epic to\n"
              f"  awaiting-answer and stop. Children: "
              f"{', '.join(expected_children) or 'none'}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    print(f"check-epic-signoff: PASS — {accepted} carries the stakeholder's reply, names all "
          f"{len(expected_children)} child item(s)"
          + (f", and was filed after the engagement reached rest at {boundary}"
             if boundary else "")
          + f"; DE8 satisfied by {elicitations[0]}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main(sys.argv))
